WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.730
Storage design determines what other
systems can do with uploaded bytes.

2
00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:08.592
Keep uploads out of application source,
template directories, executable script

3
00:00:08.592 --> 00:00:12.178
locations, and paths involved in module or
configuration loading.

4
00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:15.488
Avoid a static web directory whose
configuration could expose

5
00:00:15.488 --> 00:00:18.982
content immediately or interpret it in an
unintended way.

6
00:00:18.982 --> 00:00:22.292
Use a dedicated storage area with
generated identifiers and

7
00:00:22.292 --> 00:00:25.787
permissions limited to the operations each
service actually needs.

8
00:00:25.787 --> 00:00:28.821
A private object bucket can provide useful
separation,

9
00:00:28.821 --> 00:00:31.947
but it does not supply application
authorization automatically.

10
00:00:31.947 --> 00:00:34.430
Bucket policies, object keys, service
credentials,

11
00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.821
and signed URLs all affect access.

12
00:00:36.821 --> 00:00:39.947
Keep unpublished objects inaccessible to
normal download paths.

13
00:00:39.947 --> 00:00:42.614
When public files are required, make
publication

14
00:00:42.614 --> 00:00:45.372
an explicit transition after validation
and processing.

15
00:00:45.372 --> 00:00:49.050
Services that ingest or transform content
should not receive broad

16
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.544
access to unrelated application files or
other tenants' objects.

17
00:00:52.544 --> 00:00:54.935
Serving introduces a separate
interpretation boundary.

18
00:00:54.935 --> 00:00:57.510
Authorize the caller against the
attachment record

19
00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.268
before resolving its server owned storage
key.

20
00:01:00.268 --> 00:01:03.946
Choose the response content type from the
validated classification or

21
00:01:03.946 --> 00:01:07.900
a deliberate generic download policy, not
the original multipart header.

22
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.670
Use attachment disposition when the
product only needs a download,

23
00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:15.164
and construct the display filename with
safe header handling.

24
00:01:15.164 --> 00:01:18.475
Add the no sniff response header to
constrain browser

25
00:01:18.475 --> 00:01:21.969
type guessing in the contexts where that
header applies.

26
00:01:21.969 --> 00:01:25.003
Attachment disposition and no sniff are
delivery controls,

27
00:01:25.003 --> 00:01:28.130
not guarantees that opening a file is
harmless.

28
00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.072
Intentionally inline formats need an
appropriate active content

29
00:01:31.072 --> 00:01:33.739
model, and higher risk untrusted content
may

30
00:01:33.739 --> 00:01:36.865
warrant a separate origin without the
application's credentials.

31
00:01:36.865 --> 00:01:39.899
Set cache policy to match confidentiality;
a private

32
00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:43.026
download must not become a shared cached
response.

33
00:01:43.026 --> 00:01:45.324
Issue download links only after
authorization,

34
00:01:45.324 --> 00:01:47.347
with appropriate scope and lifetime.

35
00:01:47.347 --> 00:01:50.658
A generated storage key or an expired
application session

36
00:01:50.658 --> 00:01:54.152
does not necessarily revoke an already
issued signed URL.

37
00:01:54.152 --> 00:01:57.646
Design storage, publication, and browser
delivery together so each

38
00:01:57.646 --> 00:02:01.140
consumer receives only the authority
intended for that file.

